newscache for Debian
====================

This is not an official Debian package, so please don't file bugs in the
Debian bug tracker. Report them at https://github.com/go-while/NewsCache/issues.


Configuration
-------------

/etc/newscache.conf reads from news.blueworldhosting.com (free, no account)
over TLS and lets nobody in: every client gets "502 You have no permission
to talk". The daemon runs as a systemd service (newscache.service) and is
started on installation. It listens on port 119 (nntp) on all IPv4
and IPv6 addresses; to use another address or port, change ListenTo.

After changing the config, check it and reload the daemon:

  newscache -p -c /etc/newscache.conf
  systemctl reload newscache

-p prints the parsed configuration, or the line with an error. If a reload
finds an error, the daemon logs it and keeps the old configuration.

The sample with every option is in
/usr/share/doc/newscache/examples/newscache.conf-dist; all options are
described in newscache.conf(5).


Letting clients in
------------------

The first matching entry of the AccessList is used, so add entries before
Default.

Clients by address: add a Client entry for a host or network (CIDR):

  Client 192.0.2.0/24 {
    allow read post
    List *
    Read *
    PostTo *
    Authentication none
  }

To test from the server itself, use Client 127.0.0.1/32 (or ::1/128):

  printf 'QUIT\r\n' | nc 127.0.0.1 119

(nc is in the package netcat-openbsd) should answer "200 NewsCache ..., accepting NNRP commands".

Clients with a login: replace the Default entry with

  Default {
    allow authentication
    List !*
    Read !*
    PostTo !*
    Authentication file:/etc/newscache.users
  }

and put one line per user into /etc/newscache.users:

  user:password-hash:*:*:*:read,post

The fields after the hash are the groups the user may list, read and post
to, and the extra permissions (see newscache.conf(5)); a line needs at least
the list field. Create the hash with "openssl passwd -6" (or -1), and make
the file readable for the group news only:

  chown root:news /etc/newscache.users
  chmod 0640 /etc/newscache.users

Without login a client can then only send AUTHINFO, HELP and QUIT. Note
that "allow read post" together with an Authentication entry does not
require a login: clients that don't log in get the allowed access anyway,
and a login only adds the permissions from the file.

PAM: the package is built with PAM, and "Authentication pam" uses
/etc/pam.d/newscache (common-auth and common-account). NewsCache checks
passwords after it has switched to the user news, so pam_unix cannot check
local passwords from /etc/shadow (only root may check other users'
passwords); "Authentication unix" fails for the same reason. Use the
password file above, or PAM modules that work without root, such as
pam_sss or pam_ldap.


Logs, spool and cleanup
-----------------------

NewsCache logs to syslog (facility news), so read the log with

  journalctl -u newscache

The spool is /var/cache/newscache, owned by the user news. newscacheclean
keeps it below SpoolSize (in KBytes, 1000000 in the shipped config); the
timer newscacheclean.timer runs it daily (systemctl list-timers). Purging
the package removes the spool.


Building the package
--------------------

From a git checkout (the release tarballs don't contain debian/):

  sudo apt-get build-dep ./
  dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

The packages are written to the parent directory. The build runs autoreconf
itself, so there is no need to run ./autogen first.
